    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d510">

                <pb facs="0730" n="628" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg628"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">765.84/844: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Atherton</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-08-16T15:00:00+01:00">August 16, 1935—3
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received August 16—11:40 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>373. As reported in my 356, August 8, 1 p.m.,<anchor n="55" xml:id="d510-f1"/>
                    Eden and Vansittart arrived in Paris on Tuesday<anchor n="56" xml:id="d510-f2"/>
                    evening to conduct preliminary conversations before the arrival of Aloisi.
                    Foreign Office received last evening a report of the Wednesday conversations in
                    which Vansittart indicated that the French still harbored two illusions: (1)
                    that the issue lay mainly between the opposing policies of England and Italy,
                    and (2) that Laval’s role was to act as mediator between these points of view
                    which implied that there could be imposed arbitrarily upon Abyssinia a
                    settlement to which he, as mediator, could induce the British and Italian
                    Governments to agree. Vansittart added that the Wednesday discussions had been
                    mainly taken up in disabusing the French of these impressions and pointing out
                    the urgency for a common Anglo-French front in settlement of an
                    Italian-Abyssinian question. Foreign Office has received no report of
                    yesterday’s conversations and they have asked me to see them tomorrow
                    morning.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Atherton</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
